L006287	\section{Event validation}
L006288	
L006289	Detector data can be impacted by noise of instrumental or environmental origin~\cite{Soni:2020rbu,Glanzer:2023hzf,Soni:2023kqq}.
L006290	Noise artifacts can manifest in several ways, such as shorter-duration, broadband non-Gaussian features known as \emph{glitches}~\cite{Nuttall:2018xhi,Glanzer:2022avx}, and narrowband features known as \emph{spectral lines}~\cite{LSC:2018vzm}.
L006291	\Ac{DQ} vetting around candidate events involves multiple checks~\cite{Soni:2024kkz}, such as event validation, the \ac{DQR}~\cite{Davis:2021ecd,Virgo:2022kwz}, and (when needed) glitch subtraction~\cite{Vajente:2019ycy,Hourihane:2022doe,Davis:2022ird}.
L006292	
L006293	\ac{GW240925} was detected while Livingston was observing with \acl{BNS} inspiral range (a conventional measure of detector sensitivity~\cite{Allen:2005fk,Chen:2017wpg,LIGOScientific:2025hdt}) of $\sim\gwSepLLOBNSRange~\mathrm{Mpc}$, Virgo was observing with range $\sim\gwSepVirgoBNSRange~\mathrm{Mpc}$, and Hanford was observing with an estimated range of $\sim\gwSepLHOBNSRange~\mathrm{Mpc}$, neglecting its calibration uncertainty.
L006294	During event validation, some components of the \ac{DQR} were flagged for investigation.
L006295	At both Hanford and Livingston, excess noise was identified in an auxiliary channel that monitors environmental noise~\cite{Helmling-Cornell:2023wqe}; however, this noise occurred at sufficiently high frequencies to be well separated from the \ac{GW} signal band, and we found no evidence that it coupled into the \ac{GW} strain during the times of interest.
L006296	In addition, a glitch was identified in Livingston data $\SEPGLITCHTIME~\mathrm{s}$ after the signal in the frequency range $\SEPGLITCHFREQ~\mathrm{Hz}$.
L006297	The glitch occurred sufficiently far from the signal that it does not impact the inference of source properties~\cite{Hourihane:2025vxc}.
L006298	Thus, glitch mitigation was deemed unnecessary.
L006299	Overall, no \ac{DQ} issues impacting the observation were found for any of the detectors.
L006300	
L006301	Approximately $\SEPARTIMEDELAY~\mathrm{s}$ of Hanford strain data preceding the time of \ac{GW240925} were initially marked as non-observing, as the interferometer was briefly taken out of observing mode to make a digital configuration change to a control loop that is a part of the arm-length stabilization system.
L006302	This stabilization system is inactive when the detector is in a low-noise state and only used during lock acquisition.
L006303	The configuration-change procedure had no impact on the strain data, which remain valid for analysis during that interval~\cite{Betzwieser:2025aaa}.
L006304	
L006305	\ac{GW250207} was detected while Livingston and Virgo were observing with \acl{BNS} inspiral ranges of $\sim\gwFebLLOBNSRange~\mathrm{Mpc}$ and $\sim\gwFebVirgoBNSRange~\mathrm{Mpc}$, respectively; Hanford formally entered observing mode with a range of $\sim\gwFebLHOBNSRange~\mathrm{Mpc}$ approximately \FEBARTIMEDELAY{}~minutes later.
L006306	During event validation, one \ac{DQR} task that monitors auxiliary channels for excess noise overlapping the signal~\cite{Macas:2023zdu} was flagged for investigation.
L006307	As with \ac{GW240925}, no \ac{DQ} issues were identified for any of the detectors, as no excess noise was found in the strain data during the time of interest.
